    "answer": 1,
    "explanation": "Top-down design orders the work from the overall structure toward the details; it does not skip the details. For example, after deciding that an office needs a file server and a backup system, the designer still has to choose specific disks, network cards, and backup software. The misconception is that starting at the top makes detailed design unnecessary; the details are still designed, just later and guided by the overall plan. A top-down approach postpones detailed components until the overall design is set, but those components must still be designed later.",
